logic.description:
The primitive hardware bit type. `logic` is a single bit; `logic[N]` is an N-bit vector. This is the fundamental value type for hardware signals in Anvil.

**Example:**

```anvil
reg flag  : logic;
reg data  : logic[8];
reg bus   : logic[32];
```

int.description:
The kind keyword for an integer compile-time parameter. Used in parameter lists to declare a parameter that takes an integer value.

**Example:**

```anvil
proc P<N : int>(ep : left Ch) { ... }
type Vec<N : int> = logic[N];
```

description:
The kind keyword for a type parameter. Used in parameter lists to declare a parameter that takes a type value.

**Example:**

```anvil
proc P<T : type>(ep : left Ch) { ... }
type Vec<T : type, N : int> = T[N];
```

loop.description:
Starts an infinite loop thread inside a process. The body expression is repeated indefinitely; the loop restarts as soon as the body completes. An optional message specifier causes the loop to restart on that message.

**Example:**

```anvil
loop {
    let x = recv ep.req >>
    send ep.resp(x)
}

loop ep.rst { /* restarts when ep.rst arrives */ }
```

try.description:
A send or receive *attempt*. If the operation can proceed immediately, the success branch is taken; otherwise the `else` branch is taken.

**Example:**

```anvil
try x = recv ep.msg { use(x) } else { fallback }
try send ep.msg(val) { success } else { fallback }
```

description:
Declares a **pool**: a set of channels plus the interfaces that use their endpoints. The compiler checks that the session types of the members are compatible.

Channels are listed as `left -- right : ChannelClass`, then a mandatory `;`, then members as `name : interface(endpoints...)` or `name : <session type>`.

**Example:**

```anvil
pool my_pool {
    a -- b : ch;
    m1 : i_left(a),
    m2 : i_right(b)
}
```

Instantiates a **pool** inside a process body. Processes are then spawned against its members with `spawn Proc[pool.member]`.

**Example:**

```anvil
proc top() {
    pool p : my_pool;
    spawn worker[p.m1];
    spawn driver[p.m2];
    loop { cycle 100 >> dfinish }
}
```

offer.description:
An **offer**: waits for the messages of its arms. Several arms may be taken together, each running its body.

Arms are `x = recv ep.m => e`, `send ep.m(v) => e`, their instantaneous forms `x = irecv ep.m => e` and `isend ep.m(v) => e`, or a conjunction `{a = recv ep.m1; b = recv ep.m2} => e` (separated by `;`). Blocking and instantaneous arms cannot be mixed. With instantaneous arms only, an optional `else { e }` runs when no arm is taken.

`offer { ... } match { ... }` continues with the case for exactly the set of arms taken: `ep.m => e`, `{ep.a, ep.b} => e`, or `_ => e`. It cannot have an `else`.

Corresponds to `&( ... )` / `⋀( ... )` in session types.

**Example:**

```anvil
offer {
    addr = recv rd.req => send rd.res(*mem[addr]),
    inp = recv wr.req => set mem[inp.addr] := inp.data
}

offer {
    x = irecv ep.req => cycle 1
} else { cycle 2 }
```

description:
Defines a named **recursive block** in an expression. `recurse X` inside it restarts the block. `μ X { ... }` is equivalent.

**Example:**

```anvil
rec X {
    let x = recv ep.req >>
    if x == 1'd1 { recurse X } else { cycle 1 }
}
```
